HERO_STR_STATIC(hero_str_669c9582, "The Zen of Heroes\n\n 1. This language begins with a tribute to David Bowie.\n 2. It is made for language models; people can read it too.\n 3. A model may have read everything; each page must still explain itself.\n 4. What the eye can see is all there is: sound and vision.\n 5. There is one way to say each thing; every program sings in the same voice.\n 6. That way may not be obvious at first; the Starman already knows it.\n 7. The semantics are familiar; the syntax is new.\n 8. Turn and face the strange: every strange choice here is deliberate.\n 9. Nothing changes behind your back; every change signs its name.\n10. Every plausible mistake must become a compile error before the program runs.\n11. Unless you mark the gap with ???: an honest hole beats a confident guess.\n12. A compile-time error is an answer; a runtime error is an ambush.\n13. An error should tell you how to fix the program. Anything less is a complaint.\n14. Repeating a word is cheap; forgetting one can cost the whole program.\n15. The specification stays small under pressure; every word must earn its place.\n16. Crashes and leaks are bugs. Ashes to ashes: what Heroes takes, it returns.\n17. When pretty code and safe code disagree, safe code wins.\n18. There is no standard library; C is the man who sold the world.\n19. Never crashing in the same car: what C might keep, Heroes assumes it keeps.\n20. We can be heroes, just for one day.\n");
